      "readings": [
        {
          "role": "first",
          "ref": "Isaiah 65:17-25",
          "refKey": "Isa.65.17-Isa.65.25",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "creation",
              "hope-and-promise",
              "last-things",
              "peace-of-god"
            ],
            "image": [
              "garden",
              "vineyard"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "comfort-and-consolation",
              "joy"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 65,
              "v_start": 17,
              "v_end": 17,
              "text": "I create — I am about wholly to change the state not only of my people, but to bring a new face upon the world, which shall abide until a new heavens and earth appear, in which shall dwell nothing but righteousness. Not be remembered - That state of things shall be so glorious, that the former state of my people shall not be remembered."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 65,
              "v_start": 18,
              "v_end": 18,
              "text": "You — The church, as well under the gospel, as under the law."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 65,
              "v_start": 20,
              "v_end": 20,
              "text": "An infant — Those that were now children, shall die at a great age. But - Yet none of these things shall be of any advantage to wicked men, but if any of them shall live to be an hundred years old, yet they shall die accursed."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 65,
              "v_start": 23,
              "v_end": 23,
              "text": "With them — Is blessed with them."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 65,
              "v_start": 24,
              "v_end": 24,
              "text": "Before they call — God promised, chap. lviii, 9, to answer them, when they called: here he promises to answer the words, as soon as they should be formed in their hearts before they could get them out of their lips. While - Yea, while they were speaking."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 65,
              "v_start": 25,
              "v_end": 25,
              "text": "The wolf, &c. — God here promises to take off the fierceness of the spirits of his peoples enemies, so that they shall live quietly and peaceably together. And dust - God promises a time of tranquility to his church under the metaphor of serpents eating the dust, their proper meat, Gen. iii, 14, instead of flying upon men: it signifies such a time, when wicked men shall no more eat up the people of God. God is served with the spirit, and not by ceremonies, ver. 1- 5. The wonderful birth and benefits of the gospel-church, ver. 6- 14. Severe judgments against the wicked, ver. 15-17. The Gentiles shall be an holy church, ver. 18-23. The eternal punishment of the wicked, ver. 24."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "psalm",
          "ref": "Isaiah 12",
          "refKey": "Isa.12",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "deliverance",
              "prayer-and-worship",
              "thanksgiving",
              "trinity"
            ],
            "image": [
              "water"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "joy",
              "praise-and-exultation"
            ],
            "function": [
              "praise-opening",
              "thanksgiving-offering"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 1,
              "v_end": 1,
              "text": "In that day — When this great work of the reduction of Israel, and conversion of the Gentiles is fulfilled."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 2,
              "v_end": 2,
              "text": "God — My salvation hath not been brought to pass by man, but by the almighty power of God."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 3,
              "v_end": 3,
              "text": "With joy — Your thirsty souls shall be filled with Divine graces and comforts, which you may draw from God, in the use of gospel-ordinances. God's armies, ver. 1-5. The destruction of Babylon by the Persians and Medes, their great distress, anguish, and utter desolation, ver. 6-22."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "second",
          "ref": "2 Thessalonians 3:6-13",
          "refKey": "2Thess.3.6-2Thess.3.13",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "call-and-discipleship",
              "church-and-unity",
              "faithfulness"
            ],
            "image": [
              "bread"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "exhortation"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 3,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "That walketh disorderly — Particularly by not working. Not according to the tradition he received of us - The admonition we gave, both by word of mouth, and in our former epistle."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 3,
              "v_start": 10,
              "v_end": 10,
              "text": "Neither let him eat — Do not maintain him in idleness."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 3,
              "v_start": 11,
              "v_end": 11,
              "text": "Doing nothing, but being busybodies — To which idleness naturally disposes."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 3,
              "v_start": 12,
              "v_end": 12,
              "text": "Work quietly — Letting the concerns of other people alone."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "gospel",
          "ref": "Luke 21:5-19",
          "refKey": "Luke.21.5-Luke.21.19",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "judgment",
              "last-things",
              "mission-and-witness",
              "suffering-and-endurance"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "assurance",
              "prophetic-warning"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 21,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "Goodly stones — Such as no engines now in use could have brought, or even set upon each other. Some of them (as an eye witness who lately measured them writes) were forty - five cubits long, five high, and six broad; yet brought thither from another country. And gifts - Which persons delivered from imminent dangers had, in accomplishment of their vows, hung on the walls and pillars. The marble of the temple was so white, that it appeared like a mountain of snow at a distance. And the gilding of many parts made it, especially when the sun shone, a most splendid and beautiful spectacle. Matt. xxiv, 1; Mark xiii, 1."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 21,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "I am the Christ; and the time is near — When I will deliver you from all your enemies. They are the words of the seducers."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 21,
              "v_start": 9,
              "v_end": 9,
              "text": "Commotions — Intestine broils; civil wars."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 21,
              "v_start": 11,
              "v_end": 11,
              "text": "Fearful sights and signs from heaven — Of which Josephus gives a circumstantial account."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 21,
              "v_start": 12,
              "v_end": 12,
              "text": "Mark xiii, 9."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 21,
              "v_start": 13,
              "v_end": 13,
              "text": "It shall turn to you for a testimony — Of your having delivered your own souls, and of their being without excuse."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 21,
              "v_start": 16,
              "v_end": 16,
              "text": "Matt. x, 21."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 21,
              "v_start": 17,
              "v_end": 17,
              "text": "Matt. xxiv, 13; Mark xiii, 13."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 21,
              "v_start": 18,
              "v_end": 18,
              "text": "Not a hair of your head — A proverbial expression, shall perish - Without the special providence of God. And then, not before the time, nor without a full reward."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 21,
              "v_start": 19,
              "v_end": 19,
              "text": "In your patience possess ye your souls — Be calm and serene, masters of yourselves, and superior to all irrational and disquieting passions. By keeping the government of your spirits, you will both avoid much misery, and guard the better against all dangers."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
